# Ship Windows .exe for Plex PC users

After meaningful hub features that users need on the Plex PC (new UI panels, desktop packaging, settings that only matter in the installed app):

1. Bump `version` in `package.json` when shipping a new release.
2. Build the installer: `npm run dist:win:all` (Hub + Inno installer + Drive publish with SHA256; Companion only if its version isn't published yet). Set `NODE_OPTIONS=--use-system-ca` if Electron/npm downloads fail TLS on this machine.
   - **Companion has its own version** in `server/companion-version.json`. Do NOT rebuild/republish Companion when its code didn't change. Only when `desktop-companion/`, `companion/`, or other Companion-packaged files change, run `npm run companion:bump` (sets it to the package.json version). `dist:win:all` refuses to build if Companion files changed without a bump; `npm run companion:status` shows the state.
3. Create/tag a GitHub Release `vX.Y.Z` and upload the Hub Inno installer + portable from `release/`. Attach Companion installers from `release-companion/` **only when Companion was bumped in this release**. Attach `*-SHA256.txt` checksum sidecars. Release notes should note that no Node/Git is required to run the app, and say which Companion version is current.
4. Copy is automatic via `publish:exe` to `G:\My Drive\exe\` (or run `npm run publish:exe` after builds). It skips files already identical on Drive, keeps only the current Hub and Companion versions on Drive, and prunes old versions from local `release/` and `release-companion/`. Never leave old versions piling up in any of these folders.
5. **Never commit** `release/`, `.exe` files, or other large binaries to git.
6. Commit the version bump (and rule/README updates if any), then push `main`.

Do this at natural stop points when the Plex PC needs the new build — not after every tiny edit. Source still goes to GitHub; the Drive `exe\` folder is the easy grab for home installs alongside Releases.
